---
import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "@components/PageHero.astro";
import Section from "@components/Section.astro";
import ClauseRef from "@components/ClauseRef.astro";
import CTA from "@components/CTA.astro";
import DiagramFrame from "@components/DiagramFrame.astro";
import GovernanceStructure from "@components/diagrams/GovernanceStructure.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Governance — structure of DETIO FOUNDATION LTD | det.io"
  description="How DETIO FOUNDATION LTD is governed: members and voting, the Permanent Director seat, Protected Provisions and the Schedule 1 public-benefit lock, Reserved Matters, asset stewardship, and winding-up to charity — with clause references."
  path="/governance"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Governance", href: "/governance" },
  ]}
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Governance"
    title="A constitution engineered against capture"
    lede="DETIO FOUNDATION LTD is a not-for-profit public company limited by guarantee (ACN 699 651 771), established for charitable and public-benefit purposes, and it may apply to be registered as a charity with the ACNC. Its constitution is deliberately structured so that neither money, nor majorities, nor time can quietly redirect the foundation away from its purposes."
  >
    <p class="prose-inst mt-1 text-sm">
      Plain-language summary with clause references. The signed constitution is the
      authoritative instrument. <ClauseRef id="2.1" /> <ClauseRef id="2.2" />
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <Section
    number="01"
    eyebrow="Structure"
    title="Members, directors, and the lock beneath them"
    lede="Three tiers: a small membership with weighted votes, a board of at least three directors, and a set of Protected Provisions that neither tier can casually amend."
  >
    <DiagramFrame
      label="Governance structure diagram of DETIO FOUNDATION LTD. Top tier, members in general meeting: the Founding Member holds a permanent membership class with two votes under clauses 14.1.1 and 17.2, alongside up to two Ordinary Members with one vote each — admission requires Founding Member consent under clause 11.5. Members appoint or elect directors with consent under clause 23. Middle tier, the board of at least three directors under clause 19: the Permanent Director seat attached to the Founding Member who chairs the board under clauses 21 and 25, a Founder-Appointed Temporary Director under clause 23.2, and further Temporary Directors serving two-year terms with a maximum of ten consecutive years. A Reserved Matters gate requires Founding Member Consent under clause 18A. Bottom tier, the public-benefit lock: the Protected Provisions of Schedule 1 — not-for-profit character, no private distribution, VirtEngine, DSEMA and all Transferred Assets stewarded for public benefit, and capital unable to buy control — amendable only by special resolution plus written consent under clause 18.3 and section 136(3) of the Corporations Act. On winding up, surplus assets pass only to a compatible charity under clauses 61 and 62."
      caption="fig 01 — members → board → protected provisions: the constitutional stack"
      class="reveal"
    >
      <GovernanceStructure />
    </DiagramFrame>
  </Section>

  <Section
    number="02"
    eyebrow="Part 3 — members"
    title="Two member classes, deliberately small"
  >
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <article class="reveal rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-7">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">The Founding Member</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst mt-3 text-[0.98rem]">
          The constitution names <strong>Jonathan Amir Samuel Philipos</strong> as the
          Founding Member <ClauseRef id="11.2" />, holding two votes at general meetings
          <ClauseRef id="14.1.1" />. The Founding Member's membership and voting class are
          permanent — they cannot be relinquished, transferred, or removed except on legal
          death or where mandatory law requires <ClauseRef id="17.2" />. Incapacity alone
          does not end membership; a lawful representative may exercise membership rights,
          but never director duties <ClauseRef id="17.4" /> <ClauseRef id="21.7" />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="reveal rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-7">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">Ordinary Members</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst mt-3 text-[0.98rem]">
          The foundation may have at most two Ordinary Members <ClauseRef id="11.3" />,
          each with one vote <ClauseRef id="14.1.2" />. Admission requires a written
          application from an adult Australian resident who supports the purposes
          <ClauseRef id="13.1" />, acceptance by the directors, and prior Founding Member
          Consent <ClauseRef id="11.5" /> <ClauseRef id="13.2" />. Membership cannot be
          transferred <ClauseRef id="16.1" />, and each member's guarantee on winding up
          is capped at $444 <ClauseRef id="63.1" />.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    number="03"
    eyebrow="Part 4 — directors"
    title="A board of at least three, with one permanent seat"
  >
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
      <article class="reveal grid content-start gap-2 rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold">Permanent Director</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst text-[0.95rem]">
          The Founding Member holds the Permanent Director seat while eligible
          <ClauseRef id="21.1" /> <ClauseRef id="21.2" /> and chairs meetings
          <ClauseRef id="25.1" /> — without a casting vote <ClauseRef id="25.3" />. The
          seat never overrides mandatory law: removal and disqualification provisions of
          the Corporations Act and ACNC standards always prevail <ClauseRef id="21.8" />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="reveal grid content-start gap-2 rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold">Temporary Directors</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst text-[0.95rem]">
          At least two Temporary Directors serve two-year terms <ClauseRef id="19.3" />
          <ClauseRef id="22.1" />, capped at ten consecutive years unless members resolve
          otherwise <ClauseRef id="22.2" />. The Founding Member may appoint or nominate
          one Founder-Appointed Temporary Director by written notice
          <ClauseRef id="23.2" />; other elections and casual-vacancy appointments require
          Founding Member Consent <ClauseRef id="23.3" /> <ClauseRef id="23.4" />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="reveal grid content-start gap-2 rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold">Duties &amp; conflicts</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst text-[0.95rem]">
          Directors owe the usual statutory duties plus a constitutional duty to protect
          the Transferred Assets from private capture <ClauseRef id="29.1.7" />.
          Related-party matters involving the Founding Member must be reviewed by
          independent legal and accounting advisers and approved by non-conflicted
          directors <ClauseRef id="30.4" /> <ClauseRef id="30.5" />. Emergency
          appointments to restore the legal minimum expire within 60 days
          <ClauseRef id="23.6" />.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="prose-inst mt-8">
      Founder remuneration is itself constitutionally constrained: the Permanent Director's
      salary is capped at the lowest lawful minimum wage for the work actually performed,
      with no sitting fees and no accrued-salary or deferred-compensation mechanism
      <ClauseRef id="41.2" /> <ClauseRef id="41.3" /> <ClauseRef id="41.5" /> — a Protected
      Provision <ClauseRef id="sch 1" />.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    number="04"
    eyebrow="Clause 18 &amp; Schedule 1"
    title="Protected Provisions: the public-benefit lock"
    lede="Schedule 1 lists twenty Protected Provisions. Amending any of them requires a special resolution and the Founding Member's written consent — and even then, no amendment may make the foundation for-profit or strip its public-benefit purposes."
  >
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="reveal rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-7">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">What the lock protects</h3>
        <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-2.5">
          {
            [
              "Charitable purposes only, without private commercial interests (sch 1 item 1)",
              "Not-for-profit: no distribution of income, assets, IP, or tokens for private profit (item 2)",
              "VirtEngine, DSEMA, their patents, and all Transferred Assets held for public benefit (item 3)",
              "Economic value structurally separated from governance — capital cannot buy control (item 6)",
              "No obligation ever to issue or operate a token (item 7)",
              "Surplus assets on winding up pass only to compatible charities (item 19)",
            ].map((item) => (
              <li class="prose-inst flex gap-3 text-[0.95rem]">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" class="mt-1.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-blue-deep)" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path d="M3 8.5l3.5 3.5L13 4.5"></path>
                </svg>
                <span>{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-7">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">How amendment works — and fails</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst mt-3 text-[0.98rem]">
          A Protected Provision can only be amended if the Corporations Act's requirements
          are satisfied (including a 75% special resolution), the Founding Member gives
          written consent, and the amendment does not end the foundation's not-for-profit
          or public-benefit character <ClauseRef id="18.3" />. This entrenchment relies on
          sections 136(3)–(4) of the Corporations Act.
        </p>
        <p class="prose-inst mt-3 text-[0.98rem]">
          Any amendment that would allow private distribution, permit private use of
          Transferred Assets, or weaken a Protected Provision without meeting that bar has
          <strong>no effect</strong> <ClauseRef id="18.2" />. The only override is
          mandatory law, a court, a regulator, or the ACNC <ClauseRef id="18.4" />.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    number="05"
    eyebrow="Clause 18A"
    title="Reserved Matters: the consent gate"
    lede="Certain decisions cannot be approved or implemented by anyone — board, members, delegates, or subsidiaries — without prior Founding Member Consent."
  >
    <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        [
          ["18A.2.1", "Admitting members, new member classes, or changing member voting or quorum rules"],
          ["18A.2.2", "Appointing or filling any Temporary Director seat (outside 60-day emergencies)"],
          ["18A.2.3", "Materially transferring, licensing, encumbering, or disposing of a Transferred Asset outside the ordinary course"],
          ["18A.2.4", "Changing the technology-stewardship model, patent strategy, open-source strategy, token policy, or anti-capture controls"],
          ["18A.2.5", "Restructuring subsidiaries, including DET-IO Pty Ltd"],
          ["18A.2.6", "Mergers or transactions materially changing control of the company or the Transferred Assets"],
          ["18A.2.7", "Choosing the recipient of surplus assets on winding up"],
          ["18A.2.8", "Anything that would impair a Protected Provision, a Founding Member right, or the public-benefit lock"],
        ].map(([clause, text]) => (
          <div class="reveal flex gap-4 rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-5">
            <ClauseRef id={clause} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0" />
            <p class="prose-inst text-[0.95rem]">{text}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="prose-inst mt-8">
      Consent may be withheld at the Founding Member's discretion — but the same clause
      forbids using it for private benefit, to breach directors' duties, to cause
      insolvency, or to override mandatory law <ClauseRef id="18A.3" />. The gate is a
      brake against capture, not a steering wheel for private interest.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    number="06"
    eyebrow="Part 5 — assets"
    title="Asset stewardship: everything flows in, nothing leaks out"
  >
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
      <article class="reveal grid content-start gap-2 rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold">Transferred Assets</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst text-[0.95rem]">
          The foundation is intended to own and steward the Transferred Assets — Founder
          IP, DET-IO Pty Ltd IP and digital assets, VirtEngine, and DSEMA — for the benefit
          of humanity and the general public <ClauseRef id="31.1" />. It must seek
          ownership or sufficient licences to both programs and all their patent rights,
          improvements, and digital assets <ClauseRef id="32.1" />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="reveal grid content-start gap-2 rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold">Founder IP</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst text-[0.95rem]">
          The Founding Member must execute all instruments needed to vest Founder IP and
          digital assets in the foundation <ClauseRef id="33.1" />, including a present
          agreement to assign future IP <ClauseRef id="33.3" />. Anything that cannot yet
          be assigned is held on trust for the foundation with an irrevocable royalty-free
          licence in the meantime <ClauseRef id="33.4" />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="reveal grid content-start gap-2 rounded-card border border-hairline bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold">DET-IO Pty Ltd</h3>
        <p class="prose-inst text-[0.95rem]">
          The predecessor company may remain as a subsidiary for activities not suited to
          the foundation directly <ClauseRef id="36.1" />, under a transfer program moving
          its IP and digital assets to the foundation via separate signed instruments
          <ClauseRef id="34" /> <ClauseRef id="35" />. No restructure of it may move
          Transferred Assets away for private benefit <ClauseRef id="36.3" />.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    number="07"
    eyebrow="Part 13 — winding up"
    title="Even the end is locked"
    lede="If the foundation is ever wound up, members receive nothing. Surplus assets must go to one or more ACNC-registered charities with compatible purposes, equal or stronger non-distribution rules, and the capacity to lawfully steward the Transferred Assets."
  >
    <div class="prose-inst max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        The recipient of VirtEngine, DSEMA, and related Transferred Assets should, where
        reasonably possible, be capable of maintaining the public-benefit,
        privacy-preserving, and anti-private-capture principles of the constitution
        <ClauseRef id="62.2" />. The recipient is chosen by special resolution — a Reserved
        Matter — or failing that by the directors, or by application to the Supreme Court
        for directions <ClauseRef id="62.3" />. Distribution to members is prohibited
        unless the member is itself a qualifying charity <ClauseRef id="61.1" />.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        The foundation is established to operate for charitable and public-benefit purposes
        and <em>may apply</em> to be registered as a charity with the Australian Charities
        and Not-for-profits Commission <ClauseRef id="2.2" />. It is not currently
        registered, and this site makes no claim that it is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section number="08" eyebrow="Continue" title="Related reading">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <CTA href="/constitution">Constitution explorer — all ten topics</CTA>
      <CTA href="/foundation/public-benefit-lock" variant="secondary">The public-benefit lock, in depth</CTA>
      <CTA href="/foundation/membership" variant="secondary">Membership</CTA>
      <CTA href="/transparency" variant="ghost">Transparency commitments</CTA>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
